.TH HOLOD.TOML 5 2026-10-10 "holo 0.9.0" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
holod.toml \- Holo routing daemon configuration file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/holod.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I holod.toml
holds the settings
.BR holod (8)
needs before it starts, such as the user and group to run as, logging
and the northbound servers.
Routing configuration is not kept here.
It is managed at runtime through the northbound servers.
.PP
The file is written in TOML.
.B holod
reads it once at startup, from
.I /etc/holod.toml
or from the path given with the
.B \-c
option.
If the file cannot be read,
.B holod
prints a warning and uses the defaults listed below.
Every key is optional and defaults to the value shown, but an unknown
key, an invalid value or a TOML syntax error aborts startup.
.PP
Everything except the configuration file itself is opened after
.B holod
switches to the configured user and group, so it must be accessible to
them.
.SH TOP LEVEL KEYS
.TP
.BR user " (string, default " """holo""" )
User
.B holod
runs as after startup.
.TP
.BR group " (string, default " """holoadm""" )
Group
.B holod
runs as after startup.
It is the only group kept, and it owns the files
.B holod
creates (log files, database, gRPC Unix socket).
Its members therefore have full administrative access to the router.
.TP
.BR state_dir " (string, default " """/var/opt/holo""" )
Directory holding the following files:
.RS
.TP
.B holod.lock
Lock held while
.B holod
runs.
An instance started on a directory already in use exits.
.TP
.B holo.db
Database holding the configuration transaction history and the state
protocols keep across restarts, such as the boot counts OSPF and IS\-IS
use to protect authenticated packets against replay attacks.
It is created on first write.
.TP
.B holod.sock
gRPC Unix socket, while the gRPC server is enabled.
.TP
.BI holo\-events\- protocol \- instance .jsonl
Event recordings, when the event recorder is enabled.
.RE
.IP
The directory must exist and be writable by the configured user or
group.
.IP
To run multiple instances of
.B holod
on the same Linux host, such as one per logical router, it is
recommended to start each instance in its own mount and network
namespaces.
The network namespace gives the instance its own interfaces, routes and
IP addresses, and the mount namespace gives it a private view of the
filesystem, so its state directory and log files can stay at the
default paths.
.SH LOGGING
Log messages can be sent to any combination of journald, a file and the
standard output.
.PP
Fine grained control over log messages, such as protocol events or sent
and received messages, is provided by the
.B trace\-options
containers of the YANG modules.
.SS [logging.journald]
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default " false )
Send log messages to the systemd journal.
Startup is aborted if the journal is not reachable.
.SS [logging.file]
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default " true )
Write log messages to a file.
.TP
.BR dir " (string, default " """/var/log/holo""" )
Directory holding the log files.
It is created if missing, and must be writable by the configured user or
group.
.TP
.BR name " (string, default " """holod.log""" )
Name of the log file.
.TP
.BR rotation " (string, default " """never""" )
How often a new log file is started.
.RS
.TP
.B never
Log to
.IR dir / name ,
appending to it across restarts.
.TP
.B daily
Start a new file every day, named
.IR name . YYYY\-MM\-DD .
.TP
.B hourly
Start a new file every hour, named
.IR name . YYYY\-MM\-DD\-HH .
.RE
.IP
Dates are in UTC.
Old files are not removed.
.PP
The
.BR style ,
.BR colors ,
.B show_thread_id
and
.B show_source
keys described under
.B Message format
also apply to this table.
.SS [logging.stdout]
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default " false )
Write log messages to the standard output.
.PP
The
.BR style ,
.BR colors ,
.B show_thread_id
and
.B show_source
keys described under
.B Message format
also apply to this table.
.SS Message format
The following keys are accepted in both
.B [logging.file]
and
.BR [logging.stdout] .
.TP
.BR style " (string, default " """full""" )
Format of each message.
.RS
.TP
.B full
Single line with the timestamp, level, enclosing spans and fields.
.TP
.B compact
Shorter single line: span names are kept, but their fields are appended
after the message fields.
.TP
.B pretty
Multiple lines per message, meant for reading on a terminal.
.TP
.B json
One JSON object per line, meant for log processing tools.
.RE
.TP
.BR colors " (boolean, default " false )
Use ANSI escape sequences to color the output.
.TP
.BR show_thread_id " (boolean, default " false )
Include the ID of the thread that emitted the message.
.TP
.BR show_source " (boolean, default " false )
Include the source file and line number that emitted the message.
.SH EVENT RECORDER
.SS [event_recorder]
The event recorder writes every event processed by each protocol
instance to a file in the state directory, one JSON object per line.
Each protocol instance writes to
.BI holo\-events\- protocol \- instance .jsonl\fR,
which is truncated when the instance starts.
The recordings help developers analyze bug reports.
They are not rotated and grow with every event.
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default " false )
Enable the event recorder.
.SH NORTHBOUND SERVERS
The northbound servers are used to configure and monitor
.BR holod .
.SS [northbound.grpc]
Holo's native gRPC northbound API, used by
.BR holo\-cli .
It is always served on the
.B holod.sock
Unix socket in the state directory, and optionally on TCP addresses.
.PP
A socket left behind by a previous run is removed, and the new socket is
created with mode 0660, owned by the configured user and group.
Access to it is controlled by the file permissions alone and no password
is requested.
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default " true )
Enable the gRPC server.
.TP
.BR address " (array of strings, default " [] )
TCP addresses to listen on, in addition to the Unix socket.
See
.B TCP addresses
below.
.TP
.BR insecure " (boolean, default " false )
Serve the TCP addresses without TLS or authentication.
See
.B Insecure mode
below.
.SS [northbound.grpc.tls]
.TP
.BR certificate " (string, default " """/etc/holo/holo.pem""" )
PEM file with the server certificate presented on TCP addresses.
.TP
.BR key " (string, default " """/etc/holo/holo.key""" )
PEM file with the private key of the certificate.
.PP
When this table is present, both keys must be given.
The files are only read when a TCP address is configured and insecure
mode is off.
.SS [northbound.gnmi]
The OpenConfig gNMI service.
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default " false )
Enable the gNMI server.
.TP
.BR address " (array of strings, default " "[""0.0.0.0:9339""]" )
TCP addresses to listen on.
See
.B TCP addresses
below.
.TP
.BR insecure " (boolean, default " false )
Serve the TCP addresses without TLS or authentication.
See
.B Insecure mode
below.
.SS [northbound.gnmi.tls]
Accepts the same keys, with the same defaults, as
.BR [northbound.grpc.tls] .
.SS TCP addresses
Each server listens on every address in its
.B address
array.
An address is a numeric IP address and port, such as
.B 192.0.2.1:9339
or
.BR [::]:9339 .
Host names are not accepted.
.PP
TLS is required, using the certificate and key from the server's
.B tls
table.
Clients must send the
.B username
and
.B password
metadata keys with every request, and are authenticated against the
users configured under
.IR /ietf\-system:system/authentication/user .
Both requirements are lifted in insecure mode.
.PP
.B holod
exits if the TLS files cannot be read, or if the Unix socket or an
address cannot be bound.
.SS Insecure mode
Setting
.B insecure
makes a server accept plaintext connections on its TCP addresses
without authenticating the clients, so neither a certificate nor a user
needs to be configured.
It is meant for labs and testing, as anyone who can reach the addresses
gets full administrative access to the router.
Clients must connect without TLS, such as with the
.B \-\-insecure
option of
.BR gnmic .
.PP
Requests are attributed to
.BI unknown@ address\fR,
where
.I address
is the client's IP address.
The Unix socket of the gRPC server is not affected.
.B holod
logs a warning for each address served in insecure mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
A configuration that logs to the journal instead of a file, and accepts
gNMI connections over TCP in addition to the local gRPC socket:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
[logging.journald]
enabled = true
.sp
[logging.file]
enabled = false
.sp
[northbound.gnmi]
enabled = true
address = ["[::]:9339"]
.sp
[northbound.gnmi.tls]
certificate = "/etc/holo/holo.pem"
key = "/etc/holo/holo.key"
.EE
.RE
.PP
The certificate and key must be readable by the
.B holo
user:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# mkdir /etc/holo
# install \-o holo \-m 0400 holo.key /etc/holo/holo.key
# install \-m 0444 holo.pem /etc/holo/holo.pem
.EE
.RE
.PP
A lab setup serving gNMI over TCP without TLS or authentication:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
[northbound.gnmi]
enabled = true
insecure = true
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/holod.toml
Default location of this file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR holod (8)
.PP
https://toml.io
